STEP 1: Generate SSH key on Windows

Open PowerShell (normal user, not admin):

ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "sultantalib@windows"


When prompted:

File location → press Enter (default is fine)

Passphrase → press Enter (empty = fully passwordless)

This creates:

C:\Users\Sultan\.ssh\id_ed25519
C:\Users\Sultan\.ssh\id_ed25519.pub


✅ Verify:

ls $env:USERPROFILE\.ssh

🔹 STEP 2: Copy public key to Ubuntu VM
Option A (recommended – single command)

Run this from Windows PowerShell:

type $env:USERPROFILE\.ssh\id_ed25519.pub | ssh sultantalib@192.168.0.11 "mkdir -p ~/.ssh && chmod 700 ~/.ssh && cat >> ~/.ssh/authorized_keys && chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys"

for gitbash

cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub | ssh -i "it-engineering-all.pem" ubuntu@ec2-65-0-80-243.ap-south-1.compute.amazonaws.com "mkdir -p ~/.ssh && chmod 700 ~/.ssh && cat >> ~/.ssh/authorized_keys && chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys"



You will enter the password once (last time).

Option B (manual – if you prefer)

On Windows:

type $env:USERPROFILE\.ssh\id_ed25519.pub


Copy the full output.

On Ubuntu VM:

mkdir -p ~/.ssh
nano ~/.ssh/authorized_keys


Paste the key → save → exit.

Then:

chmod 700 ~/.ssh
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys

